1273:\begin{lem}\label{lemma:reductiontomaxweightmatching}
1274:Given two graphs $G_1=(V,E_1)$ and $G_2=(V,E_2)$, define a graph $G'=(V',E')$ as
1275:follows:
1276:\begin{compactitem}
1277: \item $V'=\{v_1,v_2\mid v\in V\}$ and
1278: \item $E'=\{\{v_1,v_2\}\mid v\in V\}\cup \{\{u_i,v_i\}\mid \{u,v\}\in E_i\}$.
1279:\end{compactitem}
1280:\smallskip
1281:Define a weight function $w\colon E'\to\NN$ as follows; let $n:=|V|$:
1282:\begin{align*}
1283:w(\{u_i,v_j\})=\begin{cases}
1284:               n & \mbox{if $i\neq j$ and $u=v$,}\\
1285:               n+1 & \mbox{if $i=j$ (and $u\neq v$).}
1286:               \end{cases}
1287:\end{align*}
1288:Let $k\in\NN$. Then, there is a set $X\subseteq V$ of size at least $k$ such that
1289:both $G_1[X]$ and $G_2[X]$ have a perfect matching if and only if the graph $G'$ has
1290:a matching of $w$-weight at least $n^2+k$.
1291:\end{lem}
1292:
1293:\begin{proof}
1294:Assume that $G'$ has a matching $M'\subseteq E'$ with $w(M')\geq n^2+k$. Let us
1295:first check that $M'$ is in fact a perfect matching: If $|M'|\leq n-1$, then
1296:$w(M')\leq (n+1)(n-1)=n^2-1<n^2+k$, which would contradict the choice of $M'$. Thus $|M'|\geq n$ but then $M'$ must have exactly $n$ edges since $G'$ has $2n$ vertices. That is,~$M'$ is a perfect matching. Now we show how to get a vertex set $X\subseteq V$ such that both $G_1[X]$ and $G_2[X]$ have perfect matchings. Let
1297:\(
1298:Y:=\{v\mid v\in V \wedge \{v_1,v_2\}\in M'\},
1299:\)
1300:that is, $Y$ is the set of vertices of $V$ whose copies in $G'$ are matched
1301:to each other under $M'$. Let $X:= V\setminus Y$. It can be easily checked that both $G_1[X]$ and $G_2[X]$ have perfect matchings; we show this for $G_1[X]$: For any $v\in X$ we know that $\{v_1,v_2\}\notin M'$, or else we would have $v\in Y$ and $v\notin X$. Thus, using that $M'$ is a perfect matching, $v_1$ must be matched to another vertex~$u_1$, which is then also in $X$, by definition. It follows that $M'$ induces a perfect matching on $G'[X_1]$ where $X_1:=\{v_1\mid v\in X\}$. Since $G_1[X]$ is an isomorphic copy of $G'[X_1]$ under the canonical isomorphism $\phi\colon v\mapsto v_1$, we get that $G_1[X]$ also has a perfect matching.
1302:
1303:It remains to check that $X$ has size at least $k$: Observe that $|X|+|Y|=n$ since every vertex of $V$ is either in $X$ or in $Y$. Each $v\in Y$ corresponds to a matching edge $\{v_1,v_2\}\in M'$ which has weight $n$ under $w$. Thus, if $|X|<k$, then $|Y|>n-k$, which implies that $w(M')\leq |X|\cdot(n+1)+|Y|n< kn + k + (n-k)n=n^2+k$, contradicting the choice of $M'$.
1304:
1305:Assume now that both $G_1[X]$ and $G_2[X]$ have perfect matchings $M_1$ and $M_2$ for some~$X$ of size at least $k$. Clearly, the size of $X$ must be even. Define a matching $M'$ of $G'$ by
1306:\[
1307:M':=\{\{v_1,v_2\}\mid v\in V\setminus X\}\cup\{\{u_i,v_i\} \mid \{u,v\}\in M_i\}.
1308:\]
1309:In other words, $M'$ is obtained by copying $M_1$ and $M_2$ to $G'$ in the obvious way and matching all leftover vertices by the edges between the copies of the same vertex.
1310:
1311:Clearly, for each vertex $v\in V\setminus X$ this adds an edge $\{v_1,v_2\}$ of weight $n$ to $M'$. From~$M_1$ and~$M_2$ we copied $\frac{|X|}{2}$ edges each, which results in exactly $|X|\geq k$ edges of weight~$n+1$ in~$M'$. Thus, the total weight of~$M'$ is $n^2+k$, as claimed.
1312:\end{proof}
