1558: one~$i$ for every color~$1 \le j \le h$, because $w_j$ can only be matched to
1559: one vertex in~$G_1$ and to one vertex in~$G_2$.
1560: This implies that $X$ contains for each~$1 \le j \le h$ all vertices from $\{w_j\} \cup
1561: V_i$ for exactly one~$i$, since $|X|\ge k=h^2$ and $|\{w_j\} \cup
1562: V_i|=h$. Without loss of generality, let $V_i\subseteq X$ for all $1\leq i
1563: \leq h$ and let $u_i$ be the vertex in graph $H$ corresponding to~$V_i$. We
1564: show that $K=\{u_1,u_2,\dots,u_h\}$ is a clique in $H$.
1565: In graph~$G_3[X]$ each color vertex~$w_j$ must be matched to
1566: its only neighbor:~$w_{j+h/2}$ if~$j\leq h/2$ and~$w_{j-h/2}$ if~$j>
1567: h/2$ (and cannot be matched to $v_{(i, j)}$-vertices).
1568:%  Assume towards a contradiction 
1569:%  that there is a pair of vertices
1570: Let~$\{u_i,u_{i'}\}\subseteq K$, with $u_i \in U_j$ and $u_{i'} \in U_{j'}$. %, that is not adjacent in~$H$.
1571: Then, note that~$v_{(i,j')}$ and~$v_{(i', j)}$ must be matched since, by
1572: construction of~$G_3$, vertex~$v_{(i,j')}$ is only adjacent to vertex~$v_{(i',
1573: j)}$.
1574: Moreover, if~$v_{(i,j')}$ is adjacent to~$v_{(i', j)}$ in~$G_3$, then $u_i$ is
1575: adjacent to~$u_{i'}$ in~$H$. %---a contradiction. 
1576: Thus overall, $K$ is a $h$-vertex clique in~$H$, as required.
1577: 
1578: To make
1579: this reduction work for any $t \geq \ell > 3$, we insert $\ell-3$
1580: additional layers of complete graphs and $t-\ell$ layers of edgeless graphs.
1581: \end{proof}
1582: 
1583: \paragraph{$c$-Factors.} We now show that \gprop{$c$-Factor}-\ML{} with $c\ge 2$ is \W{1}-hard when parameterized by $k$
1584:for $\ell\ge2$. We reduce from \MCC which is known to be \W{1}-hard when
1585:parameterized by the solution size~\cite{FHRV09}. The hardness reduction is similar to the one we use in the proof of \autoref{thm:matching}. Intuitively, since there are connected $c$-regular graphs for $c\ge 2$, we only need one layer to build a vertex selection gadget, whereas in the $1$-factor (matching) case we need two layers.
1586:
1587:\needspace{3\baselineskip}
1588: \begin{theorem}\label{thm:cfactor}
1589: For $c\geq 2$, \FactorML is \NP-hard and \W{1}-hard
1590:when parameterized by the number~$k$ of layers to select for all $\ell\geq2$ and all $t\ge\ell$.
1591: \end{theorem}
1592: 
1593: 
1594: \begin{proof}
1595: In the following, we prove that, for $c\geq 2$, $c$-\gprop{Factor}-\ML is \W{1}-hard when parameterized
1596:by~$k$ for $t=\ell=2$ and then argue that the construction is easily
1597:generalizable.
1598:As in the proof of \autoref{thm:matching}, we give a parameterized reduction from \MCC: we are given an
1599:$h$-partite graph $H=(U_1\uplus\ldots\uplus U_h,F)$ and need to determine whether it contains a clique of size $h$.
1600: 
1601:\emph{Reduction idea.}\quad The idea of the reduction is similar to the one for \gprop{Matching} in the proof of \autoref{thm:matching}
1602:but already works for $\ell=2$ in the \gprop{$c$-factor} case ($c\geq2$). The
1603:reason that we only need two layers is that we can construct connected
1604:$c$-regular graphs for $c\geq2$ of almost arbitrary size and this allows us to construct a vertex selection gadget with only one layer.
1605:
1606:%(Note that such a clique necessarily contains exactly one vertex from each set
1607:%$U_i$ and cliques of more than $h$ vertices are impossible.) 
1608:We say that
1609:vertices from $U_i$ have \emph{color} $i$. Without loss of generality, assume that
1610:$h\geq c+1$, that $h$ is even, and that $|F|\ge 3$.
1611:
1612:\emph{Vertices.}\quad
1613:For each color~$j$, $1\leq j\leq h$, we do
1614:the following: We create a color vertex~$w_j$ and for each vertex $u_i$ in $U_j$, we
1615:create a set of $h-1$ vertices $V_i = \{v_{(i,j')} \ | \ 1\leq j' \leq h \text{
1616:and } j' \neq j\}$. Note that we have one vertex in $V_i$ for each color except
1617:the color of $u_i$. Let~$W = \{w_j \ | \ 1\leq j \leq h\}$.
1618:Furthermore, for each edge $f\in F$ we create a set of vertices $V_f$ with
1619:$|V_f|=c-1$. Let $V_F = \bigcup_{f\in F}V_f$ and $V = \bigcup_i V_i \cup W
1620:\cup V_F$.
1621:
1622:\emph{Vertex selection gadget by graph~$G_1$.}\quad The vertex selection gadget is intended to make sure the vertices selected in any valid solution of the constructed \MatchML instance correspond to pairwise differently colored vertices of~$H$. The vertex selection
1623: gadget is visualized in \autoref{fig:cfactor:selection}. It is constructed as follows. For
1624:every $1\leq j\leq h$ and every $u_i$ in $U_j$ we do the following: We create a
1625:connected $c$-regular graph on the vertex set $V_i\cup\{w_j\}$. Note that this
1626:can be done as follows: We order the vertices in $V_i\cup\{w_j\}$
1627:arbitrarily and connect each vertex to the $\lfloor c/2 \rfloor$ subsequent vertices,
1628:wrapping around at the end. If $c$ is odd, then we additionally connect each vertex $v$
1629:with the vertex at position $(x+h/2) \text{ mod } h$ in the ordering, where $x$
1630:is the position of vertex $v$.
1631:Furthermore, we create a complete graph on the vertices in $V_F$. 
1632:
1686:
1687:\emph{Validation gadget by graph~$G_2$.}\quad The validation gadget is intended to make sure the vertices selected in any valid solution of the constructed \MatchML instance correspond to vertices of~$H$ that form a clique in~$H$. The validation
1688: gadget is visualized in \autoref{fig:cfactor:validation}. It is constructed as follows. For each edge
1689:$f\in F$, we do the following: Let $u_i$ and $u_{i'}$ be the endpoints of~$f$ and $u_i
1690:\in U_j$ and $u_{i'} \in U_{j'}$.  We create a complete graph on the vertices in
1691:$V_f \cup \{v_{(i, j')}, v_{(i', j)}\}$. Note that this complete graph has order
1692:$c+1$ and hence is a $c$-regular graph. Furthermore, we create a complete graph
1693:on all vertices in $W$. 
1694:
1770:
1771:By setting $k = h^2 + \frac{1}{2}h(h-1)\cdot(c-1)$ we complete the
1772:construction, which can clearly be performed in polynomial time and the new
1773:parameter~$k$ solely depends on~$h$.
1774:
1775:\emph{Correctness.}\quad $(\Rightarrow)$: Assume that graph $H$
1776:has an $h$-colored clique $K$ and without loss of generality $K=\{u_1, u_2, \ldots,
1777:u_h\}$ and $u_i \in U_i$ for each $i \in \{1, \ldots, h\}$. Let $F_K$ denote the set of all edges in the clique $K$. Furthermore,
1778:let $V_K = \bigcup_{1\leq i\leq h}V_i$ and $V_{F_K} = \bigcup_{f\in F_K}V_f$. We
1779:show that $X = W \cup V_K \cup V_{F_K}$ is a solution for
1780:$c$-\textsc{Factor}-\ML. Note that~$|X| = h^2 +
1781:\frac{1}{2}h(h-1)\cdot(c-1)$ by construction. It remains to show that $G_1[X]$ and $G_2[X]$ each
1782:have $c$-factors. Observe that for any graph~$G=(V, E)$ and any partition
1783:$\mathcal{P} = \{P_1, P_2, \ldots, P_p\}$ of~$V$ we have that, if
1784:$G[P_i]$ has a $c$-factor for all~$i$,~$1\leq i\leq p$, then $G$ has a $c$-factor as
1785:well.
1786:\begin{compactenum}
1787:  \item Note that $G_1[V_{F_K}]$ is a complete graph of order
1788:  $\frac{1}{2}h(h-1)\cdot(c-1)\geq 2c+1$ and hence has a $c$-factor.
1789:  Furthermore, let $u_i \in K$ and assume that $u_i \in U_j$. Then we have that $G_1[V_i \cup
1790:  \{w_j\}]$ is by construction a $c$-regular graph and hence also has a
1791:  $c$-factor.
1792:  Since~$K$ is $h$-colored, $\{V_i \cup
1793:  \{w_j\} \ | \ u_i \in K \text{ and } u_i \in U_j \}$ is a partition of~$V_K \cup W$.
1794:  \item Note that $G_2[W]$ is a complete graph of size $h\geq c+1$ and hence
1795:  has a $c$-factor.
1796:  Let~$f\in F_K$ be the edge connecting $u_i$ and $u_{i'}$, and let $u_i
1797:\in U_j$ and $u_{i'} \in U_{j'}$. Then we have that $G_2[V_f \cup \{v_{(i, j')},
1798:v_{(i', j)}\}]$ is by construction a complete graph of order~$c+1$ and hence also
1799:has a $c$-factor. Since $K$ is an $h$-colored clique, we have that $\{V_f \cup \{v_{(i, j')},
1800:v_{(i', j)}\} \ | \ f \in F_K \text{ and } f = \{u_i,
1801:u_{i'}\} \}$ is a partition of
1802:  $V_{F_K} \cup V_K$. Notably, we also have that $\mathcal{P}_K = \{\{v_{(i, j')},
1803:v_{(i', j)}\} \ | \ \{u_i,
1804:u_{i'}\} = f \text{ for some } f \in F_K\}$ is a
1805:partition of~$V_K$, since any $u_i$ is connected to $h-1$ other vertices with a
1806:different color each and therefore we have that $\{v_{(i, j')} \ | \ \{v_{(i, j')},
1807:v_{(i', j)}\} \in \mathcal{P}_K\} = V_i$.
1808:\end{compactenum}
1809: $(\Leftarrow)$: Assume that there is a vertex set $X\subseteq V$ such that $|X|\geq k$
1810:and $G_i[X]$ has a $c$-factor for~$\ell$ different layers $i$.
1811:By construction of the layers we have that $G_1[X]$ and $G_2[X]$ both have
1812:$c$-factors. Furthermore, we show the following facts:
1813:\begin{compactdesc}
1814:  \item[Fact 1:] \label{enum:vertices} If $v_{(i, j')} \in X$ and $u_i \in U_j$,
1815:  then $V_i \subseteq X$ and $w_j \in X$: By construction $G_1[V_i\cup\{w_j\}]$ is a connected $c$-regular
1816:  graph and each $v_{(i, j')}\in V_i$ is \emph{only} connected to other vertices in
1817:  $V_i\cup\{w_j\}$ in $G_1$. Note that any proper subgraph of a connected
1818:  $c$-regular graph is not $c$-regular and does not have a $c$-factor. It
1819:  follows that as soon as any $v_{(i, j')}\in V_i$ is included in $X$, all other
1820:  vertices in $V_i$ have to be included, as well as $w_j$, the vertex
1821:  corresponding to the color of $u_i$.
1822:  \item[Fact 2:] \label{enum:colors1} If $V_i \subseteq X$ and $V_{i'} \subseteq
1823:  X$ and $u_i \in U_j$, then $u_{i'}\notin U_j$: Note that $G_1[V_{i'}]$ does
1824:  not have a $c$-factor. By construction we get a connected $c$-regular graph by
1825:  adding the vertex corresponding to the color of $u_{i'}$, hence $G_1[V_{i'}]$
1826:  is a proper subgraph of a connected $c$-regular graph. Furthermore, we have
1827:  that $w_j$ is already part of the $c$-regular spanning graph of
1828:  $G_1[V_i\cup\{w_j\}]$, therefore $u_{i'}$ cannot have color $j$,
1829:  that is, $u_{i'}\notin U_j$.
1830:  \item[Fact 3:] \label{enum:edges2} If $X \cap V_f \neq \emptyset$ for some $f
1831:  \in F$, then $V_f \cup \{v_{(i, j')},
1832:v_{(i', j)}\} \subseteq X$, where $u_i \in U_j$ and~$u_{i'}\in U_{j'}$ are the endpoints of~$f$: By
1833:construction, $G_2[V_f \cup \{v_{(i, j')}, v_{(i', j)}\}]$ is a clique of size~$c+1$ and hence a connected $c$-regular
1834:graph. Furthermore, this clique is disconnected from the rest of $G_2$ and hence
1835:as soon as one of its vertices is included in $X$, all of them are.
1836:\end{compactdesc}
1837:Now we show that $K = \{u_i \ | \ V_i \subseteq X\}$ is an $h$-colored clique in
1838:$H$. First, we show that there must be some $v_{(i, j)}\in X$: Since $|X|\geq
1839:h^2 + \frac{1}{2}h(h-1)\cdot(c-1)$, we have that $X \neq W$ and from Fact~3 we get that $X \not \subseteq W \cup V_F$.
1840:
1841:By Fact~1 we know that any $V_i$ is either a
1842:subset of $X$ or $X\cap V_i = \emptyset$.
1843:Furthermore, if~$V_i \subseteq X$ and $u_i \in U_j$, so is $w_j$.
1844:Fact~2 yields that we cannot have two vertices of the
1845:same color in~$K$. This implies
1846:\begin{equation}\label{ineq1}
1847:|\bigcup_{V_i \subseteq X} V_i \cup W|\leq h^2.
1848:\end{equation}
1849:Note that $|X|\geq h^2 + \frac{1}{2}h(h-1)\cdot(c-1)$ and Inequality~\ref{ineq1}
1850:imply that $X\cap V_F \neq \emptyset$. By Fact~3, $X$ can only include vertices corresponding to edges between vertices $u_i$
1851:and $u_{i'}$ if $V_i \cup V_{i'} \subseteq X$. Therefore,
1852:\begin{equation}\label{ineq2}
1853:|X\cap V_F| \leq \frac{1}{2}h(h-1)\cdot(c-1).
1854:\end{equation}
1855:Note that $|X| = h^2 + \frac{1}{2}h(h-1)\cdot(c-1)$ if and only if both
1856:Inequalities~\ref{ineq1}~and~\ref{ineq2} are equalities. This implies that $|K| = h$, all vertices in $K$ have different
1857:colors and all colors are present (Inequality~\ref{ineq1}), and that $K$ is a clique
1858:in $H$ (Inequality~\ref{ineq2}).
1859:
1860: To make
1861: this reduction work for any $t \geq \ell > 2$, we insert $\ell-2$
1862: additional layers of complete graphs and $t-\ell$ layers of edgeless graphs.
1863:\end{proof}
