1865:\section{Hamiltonian Paths}
1866:\label{sec:path-hard}
1867:
1868:In this section we investigate the problem of finding Hamiltonian subgraphs, that is, subgraphs that have a simple path visiting all vertices.
1869:\autoref{cor:hardness} in \autoref{sec:meta} states that \PathML is
1870:\W{1}-hard when parameterized by the combined parameter~$k$ and~$\ell$. Through closer
1871:inspection we can get a stronger result. \gprop{Hamiltonian}-\SL is known to be \NP-hard and \FPT when
1872:parameterized by the size of the subgraph~$k$~\cite{monien1985find}.
1873:For the multi-layer case, we can show that it is already \W{1}-hard for any
1874:constant $\ell \geq 2$.
1875:
1876:\begin{theorem}
1877:\label{thm:hamiltonian}
1878:  \PathML is \NP-hard and \W{1}-hard when parameterized by the number~$k$ of vertices to select for
1879:  all $\ell \geq 2$ and $t \geq \ell$.
1880:\end{theorem}
1881:\begin{proof}
1882:We reduce from the \textsc{Multicolored Biclique} problem. In \textsc{Multicolored Biclique} we are given a bipartite graph
1883:$H=(U\cup W, F)$ and a partitioning that partitions vertices in $U$ and $W$ into $h$
1884:parts each, that is, $U = U_1 \uplus \ldots \uplus U_h$ and $W = W_1 \uplus
1885:\ldots \uplus W_h$. We call these $2h$ parts \emph{colors}, that is, each vertex of~$H$ is of exactly one color in~$\{U_1, \ldots, U_h, W_1, \ldots, W_h\}$. We need to determine whether $H$
1886:contains a biclique of size $2h$ that contains one vertex of each color. A simple parameterized reduction from \textsc{Clique} shows that \textsc{Multicolored
1887:  Biclique} is \W{1}-hard~\cite{DM12}.
1888:Observe that the vertex coloring implies that any solution contains~$h$ vertices from~$U$ and~$h$ vertices from~$W$. We
1889:will call the vertices from~$U$ \emph{low}, and the vertices from~$W$
1890:\emph{high}, imagining that the vertices from~$W$ are on top of the vertices from~$U$. Similarly, we call colors~$U_i$ \emph{low} and colors~$W_i$~\emph{high}. Note that all colors are different.
1891:Given an instance~$H$ of \textsc{Multicolored
1892:  Biclique}, we construct an instance of \PathML for
1893:  $t=\ell=2$ as follows
1894: and then argue that the construction is easily generalizable.
1895:  
1896:  \emph{Reduction idea.}\quad The main idea is to create new vertices for both the vertices of the \textsc{Multicolored
1897:  Biclique} instance as well as the edges. We create two layers where in the first one any maximal Hamiltonian subgraph contains a path that ``selects'' one vertex from each color and one edge of each combination of a high and low color. The second layer is constructed in such a way that each maximal Hamiltonian subgraph of the first layer is also Hamiltonian in the second layer if and only if the selected edges indeed connect the selected vertices of the respective colors, implying that the selected vertices form a multicolored biclique.
1898:  
1899:\emph{Vertices.}\quad The vertex
1900:set~$V$ consists of the following subsets:
1901:\begin{compactitem}
1902:  \item All vertices $U\cup W$ of $H$,
1903:\item $\{s_1,s_2\}$, where we assume that~$s_1$ and~$s_2$ are not vertices
1904:of~$H$,
1905:\item $A_{i,j}$,~$1\le i \le h$, $1\le j\le h$,
1906:where~$A_{i,j}:=\{\alpha_{\{u,w\}}\mid \{u,w\}\in F \wedge u\in U_i \wedge w\in
1907:W_j\}$, and
1908:\item $D_{i,j}$,~$1\le i\le h$, $1\le j \le h$, where
1909:$D_{i,j}:=\{\delta_{\{w,u\}}\mid \{w,u\}\in F \wedge w\in W_i \wedge u\in
1910:U_j\}$.
1911:\end{compactitem}
1912:Informally, the latter two sets are constructed by adding two vertices
1913:for each edge of~$H$, each corresponding to one orientation of the
1914:undirected edge. The vertices are then assigned to the vertex sets
1915:according to the colors of their endpoints and the
1916:orientations. Oriented edges from~$U$ to~$W$ (and their corresponding
1917:vertices in~$V$) are called \emph{ascending}, oriented edges from~$W$ to~$U$
1918:(and their corresponding vertices) are called \emph{descending}. Note that all these sets are non-empty unless we face a no-instance.
1919:
1920:Now we describe how to construct a vertex and edge selection gadget by graph
1921:$G_1$ and a validation gadget by graph $G_2$. We organize the vertices of both graphs in \emph{levels}. Each graph has~$2h^2+2h+2$
1922:levels of vertices with edges only between neighboring levels; each~$U_i$, $W_i$,
1923:$A_{i,j}$ and each~$D_{i,j}$ forms one level, the two remaining levels contain~$s_1$ and~$s_2$, respectively.
1924:
1925:\emph{Vertex and edge selection gadget by
1926:graph~$G_1$.}\quad Informally, graph~$G_1$ is constructed by putting all low
1927:vertices and their incident ascending edges into low levels, then adding two
1928:levels for~$s_1$ and~$s_2$, and then putting all high vertices and their incident
1929:descending edges into high levels. More precisely,~$U_1$ is the first level
1930:of~$G$ and~$A_{1,1}$ is the second level. Then edges are added from each~$u\in U_1$ to all vertices of~$A_{1,1}$ that correspond to an edge incident with~$u$. Then, the ascending
1931:vertices ``incident'' with vertices from~$U_1$ are added for
1932:increasing colors of the high endpoints. Afterwards, the vertices
1933:from~$U_2$ are added and then the vertices corresponding to their
1934:incident edges, and so on. The special vertices~$s_1$ and~$s_2$ are
1935:added in two middle levels, separating the levels containing low
1936:vertices from those containing high vertices.
1937:Formally, the graph~$G_1$ is constructed as follows:
1938:\begin{compactitem}
1939:\item For each~$u\in U_i$,~$1\le i\le h$, add an edge to each~$\alpha_{\{u,w\}} \in A_{i,1}$,
1940:\item for each~$\alpha_{\{u,w\}}\in A_{i,j}$,~$1 < j <h$, add an edge to
1941:each~$\alpha_{\{u,w'\}}\in A_{i,j+1}$,
1942:\item for each~$\alpha_{\{u,w\}}\in A_{i,h}$,~$1\le i <h$, add an edge to
1943:each~$u'\in U_{i+1}$,
1944:\item for each~$\alpha_{\{u,w\}}\in A_{h,h}$, add an edge to~$s_1$,
1945:\item add the edge~$\{s_1,s_2\}$,
1946:\item for each~$w\in W_{1}$, add the edge~$\{s_2,w\}$,
1947:\item for each~$w\in W_i$,~$1 < i\le h$, add an edge to each~$\delta_{\{w,u\}} \in D_{i,1}$,
1948:\item for each~$\delta_{\{w,u\}}\in D_{i,j}$,~$1\le j< h$, add an edge to
1949:each~$\delta_{\{w,u'\}}\in D_{i,j+1}$, and
1950:\item for each~$\delta_{\{w,u\}}\in D_{i,h}$,~$1\le i<h$, add an edge to
1951:each~$w'\in W_{i+1}$.
1952:\end{compactitem}
1953:The idea behind the construction is that any path from the first level
1954:to the last level corresponds to a selection of~$2h$ vertices and
1955:of~$2h^2$ edges incident with these vertices. The vertex selection
1956: gadget is visualized in \autoref{fig:hamilton:selection}.
1957:
2038:
2039:\emph{Validation gadget by graph~$G_2$.}\quad With the
2040:second graph~$G_2$ we enforce that the selected ascending and descending
2041:edges between each color pair~$U_i$ and~$W_j$ correspond to the same edge
2042:in~$H$ and that any path of length~$2h+2h^2+1$ passes through each level
2043:of~$G_1$ and each level of~$G_2$.
2044:Formally,~$G_2$ is constructed as follows. Herein, assume an arbitrary
2045:but fixed ordering on pairs of low and high colors.
2046:\begin{compactitem}
2047:\item Level~1 contains~$s_1$,
2048:\item level~$2i$,~$1\le i\le h^2$, contains all ascending vertices of the~$i$th
2049:  color pair,
2050:\item level~$2i+1$,~$1\le i\le h^2$, contains all descending vertices of
2051:  the~$i$th color pair,
2052:\item level~$2h^2+2$ contains~$s_2$,
2053:\item level~$2h^2+2+i$,~$1\le i\le h$, contains all vertices from~$U_i$, and
2054:\item level~$2h^2+h+2+i$,~$1\le i\le h$, contains all vertices from~$W_i$.
2055:\end{compactitem}
2056:All edges between consecutive levels are added except for the
2057:levels~$2i$ and~$2i+1$,~$1\le i\le h^2$: Here, we add only an edge between
2058:vertices that correspond to the same edge, that is, we add the edge
2059:set~$\{\alpha_{\{u,w\}},\delta_{\{w,u\}}\mid \{u,w\}\in F\}.$ The validation
2060: gadget is visualized in \autoref{fig:hamilton:validation}.
2061:
2140:
2141:To
2142:finish the construction we set~$k:=2h+2h^2+2$.
2143: The reduction clearly runs in polynomial time and the new
2144:  parameter~$k$ depends only on the parameter~$h$ of the
2145:  \textsc{Multicolored Biclique} instance. 
2146:  
2147:  \emph{Correctness.}\quad Thus it remains to show
2148:  equivalence of the instances. 
2149:  
2150:  $(\Rightarrow)$:
2151:  Let~$K=\{u_1,\ldots ,u_h, w_1, \ldots, w_h\}$ be a multi-colored biclique in $H$.
2152:  Let~$X$ be the vertex set containing~$K$,~$s_1$, and~$s_2$, and for each edge~$e$
2153:  of~$H[K]$, the ascending and the descending vertex corresponding
2154:  to~$e$. We show that~$G_1[X]$ and~$G_2[X]$ have a Hamiltonian
2155:  path. In~$G_1$, this path starts at~$u_1$, then
2156:  visits~$\alpha_{u_1,w_{1}}$, then~$\alpha_{u_1,w_{2}}$
2157:  until~$\alpha_{u_1,w_{h}}$. Then it visits~$u_2$ and the ascending
2158:   vertices corresponding to edges incident with~$u_2$ in the same
2159:  fashion, that is, first~$\alpha_{u_2,w_{1}}$,
2160:  then~$\alpha_{u_2,w_{2}}$, and so on. This is continued
2161:  until~$\alpha_{u_h,w_{h}}$ is visited. Then, the path visits~$s_1$
2162:  and~$s_2$. Then it visits the high vertices and the descending
2163:  vertices for their incident edges in the same fashion. By
2164:  construction, all necessary edges are present: neighboring edge
2165:  vertices correspond to edges that share one endpoint, and after a
2166:  vertex~$\alpha_i$, the next visited edge vertex is incident
2167:  with~$\alpha_i$.
2168:   
2169:  In~$G_2$, the path visits each level exactly once, going from
2170:  level~1 through level~$k$. The necessary edges are present since
2171:  the only neighboring levels that are not complete bipartite graphs
2172:  are those that contain ascending and descending vertices of the same
2173:  color pair. Since for each color pair the ascending and descending
2174:   vertex in~$S$ correspond to the same edge in~$H$, they are
2175:  adjacent in~$G_2$.
2176:
2177:  \looseness -1
2178: $(\Leftarrow)$: Observe that any vertex set consisting only of
2179:  vertices from~$\{s_1,s_2\}\cup \bigcup_{1\le i\le h} (U_i \cup W_i)$ is
2180:  disconnected either in~$G_1$ or in~$G_2$. Thus, the set~$X$ contains
2181:  at least one ascending or descending vertex. In either case it
2182:  must also contain an edge vertex of the other type: In~$G_2$, the
2183:  levels containing ascending and descending vertices alternate and
2184:  any set containing either only vertices of the first two
2185:  levels of~$G_2$ or only of vertices of the last~$2h+2$ levels is disconnected
2186:  in~$G_1$. Now, since~$X$ contains an ascending and a descending
2187:  vertex and since~$G_1[X]$ is connected, the vertices~$s_1$ and~$s_2$
2188:  are contained in~$X$. Thus, the vertex set~$X$ contains a vertex
2189:  from the first and the last level of~$2h+2h^2+2$ levels
2190:  in~$G_2$. This implies that it contains exactly one vertex of each
2191:  level of~$G_2$. Thus, the vertex set~$X$ contains exactly~$h$ low
2192:  vertices and~$h$ high vertices with different colors and it contains
2193:  for each color pair an ascending and a descending vertex. By
2194:  construction of~$G_2$ and since~$G_2[X]$ has a Hamiltonian path
2195:  visiting each level exactly once, these two vertices are the same,
2196:  that is, the~$2h^2$ ascending and descending vertices correspond
2197:  to~$h^2$ edges in~$H$. By construction of~$G_1$ and since~$G_1[X]$
2198:  has a Hamiltonian path visiting each level of~$G_1$ exactly once,
2199:  these edges are incident only with vertices of~$(U\cup W)\cap
2200:  X$. Thus,~$H[(U\cup W)\cap X]$ is a multicolored biclique. 
2201:  
2202: To make
2203: this reduction work for any $t \geq \ell \geq 2$, we can insert $\ell-2$
2204: additional layers of complete graphs and $t-\ell$ layers of edgeless graphs.
2205:\end{proof}
2206:
2207:\section{Conclusion}
2208:\label{sec:conclusion}
2209:We performed a systematic study of the (parameterized) computational complexity of subgraph detection problems in
2210:multi-layer networks. In particular, we encountered several computational hardness results
2211:for multi-layer subgraph detection problems that are solvable in
2212:polynomial time in the single-layer case. 
2213:In the following, we list
2214:some possibilities for future research with the goal to obtain positive algorithmic results.
2215:
2216:First, the case of two-layer graphs should continue to receive special
2217:attention. We showed that \MatchML is solvable in polynomial time in
2218:the two-layer case, whereas it is \W{1}-hard when parameterized by the
2219:number of vertices to select~$k$ for three or more layers. Considering
2220:acyclic subgraphs, \citet{ALMS15} also
2221:showed specialized algorithms for the two-layer case. Also \citet{CY14} focused mostly on the two-layer case. It
2222:would be interesting to systematically explore which subgraph
2223:detection problems are tractable in the two-layer case and to
2224:identify more problems that behave differently for two and three
2225:layers.
2226:
2227:Second, in many applications the input graphs are directed. One of
2228:our hardness results transfers directly to this case: The construction
2229:in the reduction from \textsc{Multicolored Biclique} to
2230:\PathML (proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:hamiltonian}) can
2231:be easily adapted to yield directed acyclic graphs by orienting all
2232:edges from lower levels to higher levels, implying that \PathML is \NP-hard and \W{1}-hard when parameterized by~$k$ for
2233:  all $\ell \geq 2$ and $t \geq \ell$ if every layer is a directed acyclic
2234:  graph.
2235:Hence, for directed acyclic graphs the complexity gap between the
2236:cases with one and two layers is even bigger because we can find a longest path in a directed acyclic graph 
2237:in polynomial-time (as opposed to being \NP-hard and \FPT with respect to
2238:subgraph order~$k$ in the undirected single-layer case). As also already mentioned by \citet{CY14}, finding positive algorithmic results for multi-layer subgraph problems in directed graphs is a challenging open direction.
2239:
2240:Finally, in some applications, one is interested in different subgraph
2241:properties for each layer~\cite{CY14,agrawal_et_al18}. For example, in
2242:the \textsc{Supported Path} problem, one layer is undirected and one
2243:layer is directed, and one aims to find an induced subgraph that is
2244:connected in the undirected layer and Hamiltonian in the directed
2245:layer~\cite{FKMR15}. There is only limited systematic
2246:investigation of the complexity of such mixed multi-layer subgraph
2247:problems~\cite{CY14,agrawal_et_al18}.
2248:
