2267:\appendix
2268:\section{Definitions}
2269:\label{appendix:definitions}
2270:\begin{description}
2271:\item[Asteroidal Triple-Free Graph] An independent set of size three where each pair of vertices is joined by a path that
2272:avoids the neighborhood of the third is called an \emph{asteroidal triple}. A graph is \emph{asteroidal triple-free} if it does not contain asteroidal triples.
2273:\item[$c$-Colorable Graph] A graph is \emph{$c$-colorable} if there is a way of coloring the vertices with at most $c$ different colors such that no two adjacent vertices share the same color.\
2274:\item[$c$-Connectivity] A graph is called \emph{$c$-connected} if it contains at least $c+1$ vertices, but does not contain a set of $c-1$ vertices whose removal disconnects the graph.
2275:\item[$c$-Core] A graph is called a \emph{$c$-core} if each vertex has degree at least $c$.
2276:\item[$c$-Edge-Connectivity] A graph is called \emph{$c$-edge-connected} if it does not contain a set of $c-1$ edges whose removal disconnects the graph.
2277:\item[$c$-Factor] A graph has a \emph{$c$-factor} if it has a $c$-regular spanning graph.
2278:\item[$c$-Regular Graph] A graph is called \emph{$c$-regular} if every vertex has degree $c$.
2279:\item[$c$-Truss] A graph is called a \emph{$c$-truss} if it is connected and each edge is contained in at least $c-2$ triangles.
2280:\item[Chordal Graph] A graph is called \emph{chordal} if each induced cycle has at most three vertices.
2281:\item[Cluster Graph] A graph is called a \emph{cluster graph} if it is a collection of disjoint cliques.
2282:\item[Cograph] A graph is called a \emph{cograph} if it does not contain any induced path of length four.
2283:\item[Comparability Graph] A graph is called a \emph{comparability graph} if there is a partial order over the vertices such that each pair of vertices is adjacent if and only if it is comparable.
2284:\item[Complete Multipartite Graph] A graph is called \emph{complete multipartite} if the vertex set can be partitioned such that each vertex pair is adjacent if and only if the two vertices are in different partitions.
2285:\item[Edgeless Graph] A graph is called \emph{edgeless} is it does not contain any edges.
2286:\item[Forest] A graph is called a \emph{forest} if it is a collection of trees.
2287:\item[Hamiltonian Graph] A graph is called \emph{Hamiltonian} if it contains a \emph{Hamiltonian path}, that is, a simple path that visits each vertex exactly once.
2288:\item[$h$-Index] The \emph{$h$-index} of a graph is the largest integer $h$ such that the graph contains at least $h$ vertices with degree at least $h$.
2289:\item[Interval Graph] A graph is called an \emph{interval graph} if an interval of the real numbers can be assigned to each vertex such that two vertices are adjacent if and only if the intervals overlap.
2290:\item[Line Graph] A graph is called a \emph{line graph} if there is another graph such that each vertex of the line graph corresponds to an edge of the other graph and two vertices in the line graph are adjacent if the corresponding edges in the other graph share a common endpoint.
2291:\item[Matching] A graph has a \emph{matching} if it has a $1$-factor.
2292:  \item[Perfect Graph] A graph is called \emph{perfect} if the chromatic number of every induced subgraph equals the size of the largest clique of that subgraph. The \emph{chromatic number} of a graph is the smallest $c$ such that the graph is $c$-colorable.
2293:\item[Permutation Graph] A graph is called a \emph{permutation graph} if the vertices represent elements in a permutation such that two vertices are adjacent if and only if the respective pair of elements is reversed by the permutation.
2294:\item[Planar Graph] A graph is called \emph{planar} if it can be embedded in the plane without edge-crossings, that is, it can be drawn on the plane such that its edges only intersect at their endpoints.
2295:\item[Quasi-Threshold Graph] A graph is called a \emph{quasi-threshold graph} if it does not contain any induced path of length four and any induced cycle of length four.
2296:\item[Split Graph] A graph is called a \emph{split graph} if its vertices can be partitioned into a clique and an independent set.
2297:\item[Star] A graph is called a \emph{star} if it is a tree with only one internal node.
2298:\end{description}
2299:\end{document}
